# Supply-chain hardening: 7-day release cooldown.
#
# Only versions that have been on the registry for more than 7 days are eligible
# for installation. This blunts the "publish malicious version -> get pulled into
# CI within minutes" class of attack (Shai-Hulud et al.), which relies on fast
# automated consumption before the version is detected and unpublished.
#
# !! REQUIRES npm >= 11.10.0 !!
# `min-release-age` was added in npm 11.10.0 (2026-02-11). Older npm silently
# IGNORES unknown .npmrc keys - it does not warn and it does not fail. On npm
# 10.x this file provides NO protection whatsoever.
#
# The `engines.npm` range in package.json enforces the floor for local installs;
# CI must also pin npm >= 11.10.0 (e.g. `npm i -g npm@^11.10.0` or
# actions/setup-node with a matching npm) or the cooldown is not applied there.
#
# Unit is DAYS (npm). Note pnpm's equivalent `minimumReleaseAge` is in MINUTES -
# do not copy this value across package managers without converting.
min-release-age=7

# First-party packages are exempt from the cooldown.
#
# The cooldown guards against a THIRD-PARTY maintainer account being
# compromised, where the value comes from letting the ecosystem detect a bad
# version during the window. @startupkit-app packages are built and published
# from our own repositories via npm Trusted Publishing (OIDC, no long-lived
# token), so their provenance is already known and the soak window buys nothing
# - it only delays consuming our own releases by a week, which blocks shipping
# a coordinated change across the SDK and this template.
#
# The trade-off, stated plainly: if a first-party repository were itself
# compromised, this exemption means the cooldown is not a backstop for it.
# That risk is accepted deliberately; it is not an oversight.
min-release-age-exclude[]=@startupkit-app/*
